import ExponentialIdentities.TwoBaseIntegerExponent.FiniteCheck16384

namespace LeanProofs.TwoBaseIntegerExponent

open Set

/-!
This file extends the exact finite verification from `16384` to `32768`: any real exponent
with `2 ^ x` and `3 ^ x` both integral and `2 ^ x < 32768` is an integer, so a nonintegral
solution must satisfy `x ≥ 15`.

For each `m` with `16384 ≤ m < 32768` the table records a pair `(a, e)`, where
`a = ⌊m ^ (log 3 / log 2)⌋` and `e` indexes `encl32768`, the list of the `37` enclosure
quadruples `(Lp, Lq, Up, Uq)` the range needs.  Each names a pair of rational enclosures
`Lp / Lq < log 3 / log 2 < Up / Uq` drawn from the continued-fraction convergent and
semiconvergent ladder.  Splitting the exponents out of the rows this way keeps the row
literals small, which matters: the elaborator holds all four chunk tables at once.  The row
is accepted when all four exact integer comparisons

* `2 ^ Lp < 3 ^ Lq`   (the lower enclosure really is below `log 3 / log 2`),
* `3 ^ Uq < 2 ^ Up`   (the upper enclosure really is above it),
* `a ^ Lq < m ^ Lp`,
* `m ^ Up < (a + 1) ^ Uq`

hold, and these together trap `m ^ (log 3 / log 2)` strictly between `a` and `a + 1`, so it
is not an integer and `3 ^ x` cannot be one either.

Unlike `FiniteCheck4096`--`FiniteCheck16384`, the enclosure facts are checked inside the same
`decide` as the row comparisons rather than being proved separately per tier.  Each row is
therefore self-contained: there is no tier `match`, no per-tier base theorem, and no
disjunctive tier spec.  The table is split into four chunks of `4096` rows, each replayed by
its own `decide`, so no single kernel evaluation has to hold the whole run.  The hardest row
is `m = 26983`, needing the enclosure `10444047 / 6589460`.

The table was produced with high-precision decimal arithmetic and every row, enclosure facts
included, was re-verified with exact GMP big-integer arithmetic before being replayed by the
kernel through `decide`.  No `native_decide` is used.
-/

